      {{!-- HOW THE SHELVES ARE ORGANIZED (the corpus owner, 2026-09-26: "a section on shelf organization that goes Latin
           Fathers (Migne), Greek Fathers (Migne), and navigation of authors for each shelf"). Written against the live
           rooms: By author / By volume, the A-Z bar, the search's three groups, the century, tradition and page-scan
           filters, the author row, the author page's tabs, and Study this shelf. No live counts in the prose, so it
           cannot go stale; the rooms show their own. --}}
      <section class="faith-tp-shelves" id="shelves" aria-labelledby="tp-shelves-title">
        <h2 id="tp-shelves-title">How the shelves are organized</h2>
        <p>The library has ten shelves. Open <a href="/the-faith-received/all-works/">All Works</a> and choose <strong>Browse the shelves</strong> to see them all, each with its best-known authors. The two largest, the Latin and the Greek Fathers, come from Migne&rsquo;s nineteenth-century editions of the Fathers, and they are laid out the way Migne printed them.</p>

        <h3>Latin Fathers: Migne&rsquo;s <em>Patrologia Latina</em></h3>
        <p>Jacques-Paul Migne printed the Latin writers of the Church, from Tertullian to Pope Innocent III, in 221 volumes. Scholars cite his edition by volume and column: <em>PL 32, cols. 657&ndash;868</em> is Augustine&rsquo;s <em>Confessions</em>. Every work on the <a href="/the-faith-received/patrologia-latina/">Latin Fathers</a> shelf carries that address.</p>
        <p>The shelf opens two ways. <strong>By author</strong> lists every author from A to Z, with dates and a count of works. Open a name to see the works in Migne&rsquo;s order, each with its English and Latin title and its volume and columns. <strong>By volume</strong> lists Migne&rsquo;s volumes in order; choose one to see what it holds. That is the quickest way to follow a citation such as &ldquo;PL 139, 473a&rdquo; back to its page.</p>

        <h3>Greek Fathers: Migne&rsquo;s <em>Patrologia Graeca</em></h3>
        <p>Migne&rsquo;s Greek series runs to 161 volumes, from the Apostolic Fathers to the fifteenth century, with a Latin translation printed in a column beside the Greek. The <a href="/the-faith-received/patrologia-graeca/">Greek Fathers</a> shelf is arranged the same way, by author or by volume. Titles are given in English and in Migne&rsquo;s Latin, and citations follow the same pattern: Gregory of Nazianzus&rsquo;s first oration, <em>On Holy Easter</em>, is <em>PG 35, cols. 393&ndash;401</em>. In the reader the column numbers mark the text, and <strong>Browse PG 35</strong> in the contents opens the rest of the volume.</p>
        <p class="faith-tp-note">The Eastern Fathers, from the <em>Patrologia Orientalis</em>, have a room of their own in the same form. The other shelves, from Medieval to Humanism and Law, open All Works with that shelf chosen.</p>

        <h3>Finding an author on any shelf</h3>
        <ol class="faith-tp-steps">
          <li>
            <h4>Jump by letter, or search</h4>
            <p>The A&ndash;Z bar shows how many authors each letter holds. The search box takes a name or a title and sorts what it finds: the matching authors first, then other works that name them, then unattributed works, such as the pieces Migne printed under Augustine&rsquo;s name whose author is uncertain.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Narrow the list</h4>
            <p>Filter by century, keep only the works with page scans, and on the Greek shelf filter by tradition. The English writers shelf also divides into Puritan, Anglican and the Westminster Assembly.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Open an author</h4>
            <p>Each name opens to that author&rsquo;s works on the shelf, and each work opens in the reader. <strong>About</strong> leads to the author&rsquo;s own page: every work, with a filter by kind, and the author&rsquo;s positions, Scripture, topics, reception and connections.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>See a shelf&rsquo;s authors together</h4>
            <p><strong>Authors</strong>, under <em>Study this shelf</em>, lists a shelf&rsquo;s authors by period, from the early Church to later writers, in library order, A to Z, by number of works or by Scripture citations.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h4>Study the shelf</h4>
            <p><em>Study this shelf</em> keeps the rest of the library on that shelf as well: <strong>Ask this shelf</strong> answers from its works alone, and Scripture, Topics and Connections open on the same shelf.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>

